---
title: Native constraints and custom validity
description: Combine HTML constraint attributes with server-side schema rules and mirror server errors into the browser's native validity bubble.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

Browsers check `required`, `minlength` and friends before a form ever submits — instant, free feedback. But a request can arrive without a browser, so the schema must repeat every rule, plus the ones HTML cannot express. Superforms' `customValidity` option then copies server errors back onto the control's native validity message, so the browser's own bubble agrees with the rendered errors.

## Schema

The handle is constrained twice on purpose: HTML attributes cover the browser, the schema repeats those rules and adds the reserved-name check no attribute can express.

```ts title="src/routes/handle/schema.ts"
import { z } from "zod/v3";

export const constraintsSchema = z.object({
	handle: z
		.string()
		.min(3, "Use at least three characters.")
		.max(12, "Use at most twelve characters.")
		.regex(/^[a-z0-9_]+$/, "Use lowercase letters, digits and underscores.")
		.refine((value) => value !== "admin", "This handle is reserved."),
	invite: z.string().regex(/^INV-\d{3}$/, "Enter an invite code such as INV-123."),
});

export type ConstraintsData = z.infer<typeof constraintsSchema>;
```

## Server

```ts title="src/routes/handle/+page.server.ts"
import { fail } from "@sveltejs/kit";
import { message, superValidate } from "sveltekit-superforms";
import { zod } from "sveltekit-superforms/adapters";
import { constraintsSchema } from "./schema.js";

export const load = async () => ({
	form: await superValidate(zod(constraintsSchema)),
});

export const actions = {
	default: async ({ request }) => {
		const form = await superValidate(request, zod(constraintsSchema));

		if (!form.valid) return fail(400, { form });

		return message(form, `Saved the handle ${form.data.handle}.`);
	},
};
```

## Form

`customValidity: true` is what mirrors each field's server errors onto its control's native validity message. The `invite` field opts out with `data-no-custom-validity`.

```svelte title="src/routes/handle/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Field, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { ConstraintsData } from "./schema.js";

	let { data }: { data: { form: SuperValidated<ConstraintsData> } } = $props();

	const form = superForm(
		untrack(() => data.form),
		{ customValidity: true, resetForm: false }
	);
	const { form: formData, enhance, message } = form;
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
	<Field {form} name="handle" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Handle</Label>
				<input
					{...props}
					required
					minlength={3}
					maxlength={12}
					pattern="[a-z0-9_]+"
					bind:value={$formData.handle}
				/>
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}
			Lowercase letters, digits and underscores, 3–12 characters.
		{/snippet}
	</Field>

	<Field {form} name="invite" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Invite code</Label>
				<input {...props} required data-no-custom-validity bind:value={$formData.invite} />
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}An INV-123 shaped code, enforced on the server.{/snippet}
	</Field>

	<button type="submit">Save handle</button>
</form>

{#if $message}
	<output>{$message}</output>
{/if}
```

## How it works

- HTML attributes (`required`, `minlength`, `maxlength`, `pattern`) are checked by the browser before submission; the schema repeats them because client-side checks are bypassable, and adds the reserved-name `refine` that no attribute can express.
- `Control` derives `aria-required` from the schema's constraints, so the state announced to assistive technology matches the browser's required state.
- With `customValidity: true`, a server error on `handle` (for example the reserved-name message) appears both in the rendered field errors and in the browser's native validation bubble.
- `data-no-custom-validity` on the `invite` input skips that mirroring: its server errors still render through `fieldErrors`, `aria-invalid` still reflects them, and the built-in `required` check still applies — only the native message assignment is skipped.

<Callout type="warning">

Do not call `setCustomValidity` yourself on a control that Superforms manages. Processing field changes can clear a caller-set message, so your custom text is not guaranteed to survive. Use `data-no-custom-validity` to opt a control out instead of fighting the assignment.

</Callout>

<Callout type="note">

Native constraint bubbles are a browser UI — they are not announced the way `FieldErrors` live regions are, and they only appear on submit attempts. Keep `fieldErrors` enabled so every user gets the same messages; see [Accessibility](/docs/guides/accessibility).

</Callout>
